+            subprocess.run([os.environ.get("CC", "cc"), "-std=gnu11",
+                            "-Wall", "-Wextra", "-Werror",
+                            "-Wno-unused-parameter", "-O1", "-g",
+                            "-fsanitize=address,undefined",
+                            "-fno-sanitize-recover=all", str(cfile),
+                            "-o", str(binary)], check=True)
+            env = os.environ.copy()
+            env.setdefault("ASAN_OPTIONS", "detect_leaks=0")
+            subprocess.run([str(binary)], check=True, env=env)
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    main()
diff --git a/tools/tests/encfs_manager/README.md b/tools/tests/encfs_manager/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..65614b283f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/tests/encfs_manager/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# EncFS manager lifecycle tests
+
+From any working directory on a POSIX host with a C compiler and pthreads:
+
+```sh
+nuttx/tools/tests/encfs_manager/run_host_tests.sh
+```
+
+The runner compiles the production `encfs_manager.c`, `encfs_key.c`, and
+`encfs_block_port.c` with warnings treated as errors, AddressSanitizer, and
+UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. It reads the real public API headers. The checked-in
+shims provide a host configuration and replace external board, block-device,
+key-slot, and VFS dependencies with an observable model. HKDF itself is stubbed
+to check the exact 32-byte input/output lengths, ASCII salts without their NUL,
+and empty info. This suite tests orchestration and rollback, not cryptographic
+correctness or real filesystem I/O; `../encfs_crypto` and the Guest acceptance
+program cover those separately.
+
+The cases exercise absent and invalid keys; every one-shot startup failpoint;
+retry after failure; repeated start and stop; runtime key replacement refusal;
+busy file/directory unmount behavior; failed upper authentication; the VFS path
+where bind succeeds but publication fails; lower-unmount and block-unregister
+cleanup errors; retrying cleanup without reporting false STOPPED; and explicit
+provisioning versus a normal start on an unformatted volume. Upper target
+reservation is checked for allocation failure and an existing foreign mount.
+
+The manager also reconciles its recorded mount identities against the actual
+inode tree. After a direct external unmount of `/data`, status changes from
+`READY` to `BLOCK_KEYED`; start returns `-EBUSY` until stop releases the remaining
+block device, and the next start mounts the volume normally. The suite covers
+both a missing upper path and a surviving pseudo directory, an externally
+unmounted lower during cleanup, and replacement mounts at either path. Stop
+preserves replacements, including simulated reuse of inode/private-data
+addresses with a different inode serial. Identity checks and unmount run under
+the same inode tree lock so replacement cannot occur between those operations.
+
+The test creates only a temporary host executable and never accesses a real
+block device. It has no network dependencies. `HOSTCC` selects a host compiler.
+`ENCFS_MANAGER_SANITIZERS=undefined` selects UBSan only, and
+`ENCFS_MANAGER_SANITIZERS=none` disables sanitizers. Leak detection is disabled
+by default because ptrace-based sandboxes cannot run LeakSanitizer; the test
+performs no dynamic allocations. Set `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1` to override
+that default outside such a sandbox.
diff --git a/tools/tests/encfs_manager/run_host_tests.sh b/tools/tests/encfs_manager/run_host_tests.sh
new file mode 100755
index 0000000000..93ce939dcc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/tests/encfs_manager/run_host_tests.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+set -euo pipefail
+
+test_dir=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)
+nuttx_dir=$(cd -- "$test_dir/../../.." && pwd)
+build_dir=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/encfs-manager.XXXXXXXX")
+trap 'rm -rf -- "$build_dir"' EXIT
+
+host_cc=${HOSTCC:-cc}
+flags=(-std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wno-unused-parameter -g -pthread)
+if [[ ${ENCFS_MANAGER_SANITIZERS:-address,undefined} != none ]]; then
+  flags+=("-fsanitize=${ENCFS_MANAGER_SANITIZERS:-address,undefined}")
+fi
+
+# -idirafter keeps the host standard headers ahead of NuttX libc headers.
+# The shims substitute only the external board/VFS/driver dependencies.
+"$host_cc" "${flags[@]}" -I"$test_dir/shim" -idirafter "$nuttx_dir/include" \
+  "$test_dir/test_manager.c" \
+  "$nuttx_dir/fs/encfs/encfs_manager.c" \
+  "$nuttx_dir/fs/encfs/encfs_key.c" \
+  "$nuttx_dir/fs/encfs/encfs_block_port.c" \
+  -o "$build_dir/test_manager"
+
+# LeakSanitizer cannot run under ptrace-based sandboxes. There is no dynamic
+# allocation in this test. Users can override ASAN_OPTIONS in a normal shell.
+ASAN_OPTIONS=${ASAN_OPTIONS:-detect_leaks=0} \
+UBSAN_OPTIONS=${UBSAN_OPTIONS:-halt_on_error=1} \
+  "$build_dir/test_manager"
